==Finnish==

===Etymology===
from a {{etyl|gem|fi}} language (compare Swedish ''{{term|lang=sv|själ}}'', German ''{{term|lang=de|Seele}}'' and English ''{{term|lang=en|soul}}''), or, most likely, from Baltic *''sḗjlā'' (compare Lithuanian ''{{term|lang=lt|siela}}''), along with other Finnish loanwords from Baltic languages of the same thematic group, e. g., ''{{term|lang=fi|taivas}}''  < from Baltic *''Dḗivas'' (compare Lithuanian ''{{term|lang=lt|Dievas}}'', Latvian ''{{term|lang=lv|Dievs}}'', Latgalian ''{{term|lang=ltg|Dīvs}}'' 'God'), ''{{term|lang=fi|jumal}}'' < from Baltic *''Jumala'' (compare Latgalian/Latvian ''{{term|lang=ltg|Jumala}}'' 'goddess of harvest and fertility').

===Noun===
{{fi-noun}}

# [[soul]]
# [[moving spirit]] {{gloss|one who provides significant impetus or guidance in a given venture, movement, enterprise etc.}}

